> I missed the thread in which this was decided (got a link?), 

It's a general game design principle that I brought with me from past
experiences on both the design and test front of other games.  I am not 
the originator of the concept, but it is an eminently effective principle 
to follow if you want a balanced game as the end-result.

To illustrate:  Assume there exists a scroll, I WIN.  Reading it causes
the Amulet of Yendor to appear in your inventory, you to be teleported
to the High Altar of your god on the Astral Plane, and the turn counter 
to reset so that it's your move next, before any monsters can act.

Nobody would say this scroll is balanced.  Making it only show up once
every 1,000 games doesn't make it any more balanced, it just makes it a
random broken effect.  

This is an extreme example, but it scales quite effectively; take the 
"scrolls of genocide should cost more charges" comment you made above.  
It doesn't make geno any less of a problematic effect, nor does it make 
it significantly less likely that someone is going to geno L or h; it 
just means that someone will write only a scroll of genocide with that 
marker, rather than genocide and a few enchant armor scrolls.

> have serious issues with the reasoning.  Random is -good-, for it
> gives individual games more character.  

In my experience, players do not agree with this when taken to its
logical conclusion, and would rather phrase it as "Random good events
are good and give games more character; random bad events are lame."
(This is why we see some very silly YANIs posted here that can all be
rephrased as "X is hard; there should be (item) to make X easy.")

To balance random, big bad has to be roughly as common as big good.  
"Demogorgon appeared out of nowhere on DL6 and killed me" is not fun; 
look at how many people complain about OOD monsters in Crawl and 
other games?  (You have to read rec.games.roguelike.misc, hang out in 
##crawl or #nethack, etc., to do this; there will probably not be any 
good examples of OOD Crawl critters in RGRN.)

> "That was the game in which I found a scroll of genocide on level 3" 
> is more memorable than "That was the game I found a balanced selection 
> of items relevant to my power level."  

Nobody is suggesting that there shouldn't be "very good" and "very bad"
occurrences; it's just that when something is "too good", then it's
"too good", and you cannot make it less "too good" by just making it
more of a rare occurrence.

> If a player wins because he was extremely lucky that one game, that's 
> not a bad thing.  

While this is true, no single effect should allow the player to be so
lucky -- and certainly no single effect that must only be repeated over 
and over.  Full Pw recharge effects should cost something to use.  The
current one (confused scroll of charging) actually _does_ cost something; 
the scroll of charging itself, which most players rarely ever have
a surplus of due to recharging wands of death, polymorph, wishing, etc.
It also isn't convenient to use in the midst of battle, given that the
player must confuse himself, read the scroll, and then unconfuse himself
before being able to effectively make use of the Pw -- that's three
turns minimum, probably four since you'll have to pull the scroll out of
your bag (unless you're flyin' risky with fire traps).

Compare to a blessed tin of manasucker, which requires no confusion,
only a single turn, and isn't destroyable by fire traps, water blanking,
etc. etc.  And worse, all you need to do is reverse geno once to get a
bunch of these?  Ugh.  Relying on players not having a tinning kit (the
original objection) or spare scroll of geno is just not going to work.

> I'd say that overall, the player
> should have to rely more on randomly-generated stuff, and less on
> things he can get every time by making or Castle-wishing for them, or
> finding them guaranteed.

I agree.  But this is completely different from the principle above,
which is that "making broken effects rare doesn't fix them, it just 
makes them random".

